WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:04.330
Hope you've got your happy face on, 'cause
Joker is here to bring a little maniacal mirth

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:06.420
to the gritty, mean streets of Gotham.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:11.140
Joaquin Phoenix is undeniably the star, but
he's joined by an impressive supporting cast

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:14.260
and yes, you've absolutely seen most of their
faces before.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:19.140
Her perfectly coiffed hair is on the verge
of iconic status in its own right, and her

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:23.230
name is undeniably fun to say, but it's Zazie
Beetz's seemingly effortless charisma and

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:26.410
incisive approach to her characters that's
helped set her apart.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:30.690
That combination of attributes undoubtedly
helped drive director Todd Phillips to cast

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:35.399
the gifted actor as Arthur Fleck's scrappy
neighbor and romantic interest Sophie in Joker,

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.900
though Beetz's track record of holding her
own in the company of serious actors didn't

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:39.400
hurt.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.920
That particular skill has been well on display
throughout Beetz's still-young career.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:46.519
If you're wondering where you've seen her
before, Beetz featured prominently in the

00:00:46.519 --> 00:00:51.109
"Brewery Brothers" storyline of Joe Swanberg's
egregiously underrated Netflix anthology series

00:00:51.109 --> 00:00:51.620
Easy.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:56.200
She's also played Van, the no-nonsense baby
mama to Donald Glover's Earn on the critically

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.080
adored FX dramedy Atlanta.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:02.600
It's most likely, however, that you saw Beetz
hamming it up as Domino a.k.a. the luckiest

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.719
woman alive opposite Ryan Reynolds' foul-mouthed
anti-hero in Deadpool 2.

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:07.720
"Meet…"

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:08.280
"Domino."

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:09.380
"What's your shtick?"

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:10.500
"I'm lucky."

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.340
"Luck isn't a superpower."

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:13.440
"Yeah, it is."

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:13.940
"No, it isn't."

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.360
Beetz is set to reprise that scene-stealing
role in the still-developing Deadpool spinoff

00:01:18.369 --> 00:01:22.189
X-Force, but if you don't want to wait that
long, you can currently see her in Steven

00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:26.620
Soderbergh's Netflix drama High Flying Bird
and the creepy new streamer Wounds.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:30.990
As Joker spends more than a little bit of
time examining the struggles of a life in

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:35.220
stand-up comedy, it seems fitting that Marc
Maron would feature prominently in the narrative.

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:40.340
He has, after all, spent the bulk of his professional
life making a name for himself as one of the

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:41.939
more insightful comedians around.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.420
It's also a safe bet his hard-earned success
eventually led him into direct contact with

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:50.520
sleazy late-night TV producers like the one
he plays in Joker, Ted Marco.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:54.880
Of course, the infamously cantankerous comic
has stretched his talents far beyond the stand-up

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.440
stage of late, and made a proper actor of
himself.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:02.240
Maron has stolen scenes in supporting roles
on comedic series like Girls and he even managed

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:04.460
to score his own show, IFC's Maron.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:08.560
Most recently, Maron has gotten cozy with
Netflix through a brilliantly serio-comic

00:02:08.569 --> 00:02:13.340
turn as a floundering graphic novelist in
Joe Swanberg's Easy, and very nearly stolen

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:18.230
the entire show as the faded, B-movie director
in the streaming giant's lauded "lady wrestlers"

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:18.920
series Glow.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:22.440
If you're wondering where you've seen him
on the big screen, Maron delivered a memorable

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:27.370
cameo in 2016's comic farce Mike and Dave
Need Wedding Dates, though savvy cinephiles

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:31.819
may also know him as the angry promoter who
tried to lock the gates on Stillwater in Cameron

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:34.280
Crowe's 2000 masterpiece Almost Famous.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.299
Over the past couple of decades, Frances Conroy
has essentially become a career mother on

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:40.640
big screens and small.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:44.840
She's also managed to turn every mom she's
played into cunningly complex characters you

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:45.760
can't stop watching.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.860
As such, Conroy was sort of a perfect fit
to portray Arthur Fleck's bedraggled mother

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:50.660
Penny.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:54.460
While Conroy is sure to bring some serious
dramatic energy to the relentlessly bleak

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:58.910
world of Todd Phillips' Joker, every performance
in the movie will almost certainly be overshadowed

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:00.600
by the work of star Joaquin Phoenix.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:05.340
That's okay, because even if Frances Conroy
isn't stealing the show in Joker, she's already

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:10.310
stolen our hearts as the passionately low-key
matriarch of the Fisher clan in HBO's "life,

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:12.940
death, and everything in between" drama Six
Feet Under.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:17.180
If you somehow managed to miss Conroy's work
on that series, it's entirely possible you

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:21.860
recognize her from her work as Alex and Valerie's
excessively flighty mom in Jason Reitman's

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:26.120
tragically overlooked Hulu series Casual,
though it's far more likely you know her as

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:28.220
Barney's mom Loretta from How I Met Your Mother.

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:32.220
And if you've somehow not seen Conroy in any
of those series, you should absolutely know

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:36.690
her from her multiple roles in Ryan Murphy's
horror anthology American Horror Story, or

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:40.460
perhaps from her appearance in Hulu's Stephen
King-centric series Castle Rock.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:41.980
"Penny's driving me to Portland.

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:43.040
I'm buying new shorts.

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:47.240
I told her, 'Dale retires, we're gonna find
out once and for all if he's got knees."

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.440
We suppose it's acceptable that most viewers
likely don't know Shea Whigham's name.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:56.780
But in reality, you should be far more familiar
with Whigham, as he's one of the preeminent

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:58.159
character actors in Hollywood.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:02.360
While his work as Detective Burke in Joker
doesn't seem to require much stretching for

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:07.129
Whigham he's played many, many detective types
over the years the actor still manages to

00:04:07.129 --> 00:04:11.440
put his own peculiar stamp on a character
that otherwise might've felt very stock.

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.460
That's sort of Whigham's M.O., for the record.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:17.520
Even if you don't yet recognize his name,
he's made a career of bringing uncommon depth

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:21.790
to fringe characters in all manner of film
and television projects since breaking through

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:26.230
in the early 2000s, and it's all but impossible
you haven't seen his face before.

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:30.610
In fact, you've probably seen it a dozen times
or so as the actor has brought his formidable

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:35.230
presence to indie gems like Joel Schumacher's
Tigerland, David Gordon Green's All the Real

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:37.300
Girls, and Jeff Nichols' Take Shelter.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:42.940
He's also appeared in lauded TV series like
Boardwalk Empire, Agent Carter, Fargo, True

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:47.280
Detective, Vice Principals, and Homecoming,
all while lending his talent to high-profile

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:52.320
fare like the Fast &amp; Furious franchise, The
Wolf of Wall Street, American Hustle, Kong:

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:53.900
Skull Island, and First Man.

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:57.360
So yeah, if you don't yet know Shea Whigham's
name, now's the time to learn it.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:02.220
On the subject of faces you no doubt recognize
but names you may not know, you should probably

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:04.740
go ahead and learn Bryan Tyree Henry's name
as well.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:09.620
Henry has been busy, busy, busy of late, earning
solid reviews for his work in film projects

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:14.760
like If Beale Street Could Talk, Person to
Person, Hotel Artemis, White Boy Rick, and

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:15.260
Widows.

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:20.000
Not to mention, he also contributed his voice
to 2018's Oscar-winning hit Spider-Man: Into

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:20.840
The Spider-Verse.

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:25.600
Henry has also appeared in critically hailed
TV series like Boardwalk Empire, The Knick,

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:29.949
Vice Principals, This Is Us, Room 104, and
BoJack Horseman.

00:05:29.949 --> 00:05:34.140
Of course, Bryan Tyree Henry is undeniably
best known for his role as hustling hip-hop

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:39.770
star in the making -- Alfred "Paper Boi" Miles
in Donald Glover's hilarious and often soul-crushing

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:41.480
FX series Atlanta.

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:46.110
And if you haven't seen Henry's work in Atlanta,
we'd urge you to go watch the season 2 episode

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:47.100
"Woods" immediately.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:47.780
We'll wait.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:49.340
"You're wasting time.

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:53.440
And the only people who got time are dead.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:57.180
And if you're dead, I'm gonna take them shoes
... "

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:01.680
Of all the supporting characters that have
come and gone in the Batman cinematic universe,

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:03.980
few of them have been more important than
Thomas Wayne.

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:07.520
It stands to reason that the actor who landed
the role of the Dark Knight's daddy would

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:11.480
need to be more than capable of bringing a
certain degree of gravitas to the character.

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:15.820
As portrayed in Joker, that character required
a bit of duplicity as well, as the would-be

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:20.130
Mayor Wayne may have more to do with fracturing
the psyche of the burgeoning Joker than one

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:20.620
would think.

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:24.540
Phillips and his casting director clearly
understood that when they cast veteran character

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:26.520
actor Brett Cullen as Thomas Wayne.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:31.110
Cullen's career has seen countless TV appearances
in everything from The Thorn Birds to The

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:33.740
West Wing, from Friday Night Lights to True
Detective.

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:37.940
That's not even mentioning his steady stream
of big-screen ventures, which includes Wyatt

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:42.900
Earp, Apollo 13, National Security, Ghost
Rider, and The Dark Knight Rises.

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:47.860
Before he even reaches his tenth birthday,
youngster Dante Pereira-Olson is about to

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:51.120
claim his third role as the younger version
of an adult character.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:55.160
Needless to say, Young Bruce Wayne is the
actor's biggest role yet, even if he doesn't

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:57.300
actually get to portray the Batman in Joker.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:01.960
If you're wondering what the other two roles
were, you should know that prior to Joker,

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:05.800
Pereira-Olson's most notable "Young so-and-so"
appearance was as the younger version of the

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:07.160
actor who plays the Joker himself.

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:11.820
Yep, that was Dante Pereira-Olson playing
the young version of Joaquin Phoenix's Joe

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:14.720
in 2018's harrowing drama You Were Never Really
Here.

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:18.830
He also lent his voice to the young version
of Jason Schwartzman's Bradford Sharpe in

00:07:18.830 --> 00:07:20.880
the Amazon series Mozart In The Jungle.

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:25.640
And it just so happens that Joker is hardly
Pereira-Olson's first foray into the superhero

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:30.440
genre, as he's already logged time in Netflix's
Jessica Jones, and can be seen as the youngest

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:33.820
Scaramucci in Syfy's adaptation of Grant Morrison's
Happy!.

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:38.480
Regarding the manservant who actually raised
the future Batman in the tragic absence of

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:43.120
Thomas and Martha Wayne, Alfred Pennyworth
may well be the single most important figure

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:46.670
in the long,tortured history of the Dark Knight
who isn't actually a Wayne.

00:07:46.670 --> 00:07:50.860
It also makes his presence in Joker as big
a deal as that of Young Bruce or Thomas.

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:55.380
Luckily, Douglas Hodge is the sort of actor
who can bring some serious weight to any role.

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:59.220
Though he's only recently popped up on American
viewers' radar, the British thespian has been

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:01.440
acting in various projects for decades now.

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:05.480
If you're wondering why Joker's Alfred looks
so familiar, it's likely because you saw him

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:10.780
trading lines with Hugh Laurie, Tom Hiddleston,
Elizabeth Debicki, and Olivia Colman in 2016's

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:15.410
AMC miniseries The Night Manager or you saw
him facing off against Jennifer Lawrence in

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:16.920
the spy thriller Red Sparrow.

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:21.860
Of course, Black Mirror fans will almost certainly
recognize Hodge as Rolo Haynes, the sleaze-peddling

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:25.800
proprietor of the titular roadside attraction
in the "Black Museum" episode.

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:31.180
"A lot of folks just rush behind the curtain,
hardly even eyeball this stuff, and then it's

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:35.100
a bunch of one-star ratings on Trip-f---er,
and before you know it — "

00:08:35.540 --> 00:08:39.579
If you only remember two faces and/or names
from this list, you should definitely remember

00:08:39.579 --> 00:08:43.520
those of Zazie Beetz and Brian Tyree Henry,
because they're about to become two of the

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:45.440
most sought-after talents in Tinseltown.

00:08:45.769 --> 00:08:49.800
If you have room for a third name, you could
do a lot worse than noting Bill Camp's he's

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:54.589
been one of the most consistently overlooked
talents in film and TV for a couple of decades

00:08:54.589 --> 00:08:59.019
now, and his supporting role as Detective
Garrity in Joker is not likely to raise his

00:08:59.020 --> 00:09:00.329
profile very much.

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:04.139
That being said, we'd be surprised if you
didn't recognize Camp's face, because he's

00:09:04.139 --> 00:09:08.240
also been one of the most prolific supporting
actors around in recent years, and even though

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:12.860
his dynamite character work is frequently
overlooked, his face has remained quite visible.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:17.300
As for where you've seen Bill Camp before,
he's appeared in films like Rounders, Reservation

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:22.220
Road, and Public Enemies, but had a bit of
a breakout year in 2012, delivering memorable

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:26.839
turns in the unnerving indie drama Compliance
and playing a dastardly law man opposite Tom

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:30.060
Hardy, Shia LeBeouf, and Jessica Chastain
in Lawless.

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:34.340
Camp has been keeping busy since, turning
up in awards-friendly fare like Lincoln, 12

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:36.760
Years a Slave, and The Killing of a Sacred
Deer.

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:41.400
He's also scored raves for his small-screen
work on Damages, The Night Of, Boardwalk Empire,

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:42.780
and The Leftovers.

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:47.740
You can count the perpetually deadpan performer
Josh Pais as one of the most oft-overlooked

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:49.000
talents in the film world.

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:53.750
You can also count us as excited about pretty
much any film Pais lends his talents to, because

00:09:53.750 --> 00:09:58.410
he always manages to bring an odd mix of intellect
and anxious energy to any movie he's in.

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:02.529
It should come as a sort of anti-spoiler alert,
then, that Pais brings the same off-kilter

00:10:02.529 --> 00:10:07.160
energy to his role as the pseudo-nihilistic
manager who runs Arthur Fleck's run-down hotel

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:07.800
in Joker.

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:12.000
That oddball, outsider energy shows through
in virtually every performance he's delivered

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.720
since his first credited appearance on The
Cosby Show.

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:19.810
That includes his work in Star Trek: Deep
Space Nine, Sex and the City, A Civil Action,

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:25.610
Scream 3, A Beautiful Mind, The Sopranos,
Adventureland, Law &amp; Order, Maniac, and much,

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:26.120
much more.

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:29.720
But if you want to see Josh Pais at his best,
you're gonna want to check him out in Lynn

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:35.000
Shelton's pitch-perfect 2013 indie flick Touchy
Feely, which also features stellar work from

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:37.720
Rosemarie DeWitt, Ellen Page, and Scoot McNairy.

